---
import "../styles/globals.css";
import SEO from "../components/SEO.astro";

const siteUrl = "https://zentype.jonathanrreed.com";
const aboutUrl = `${siteUrl}/about/`;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<SEO
			title="Zen Typer Background and Private Typing Practice"
			description="Who built Zen Typer, why the typing practice app exists, and how it keeps mindful typing account-free with everything stored in your browser."
			canonical={aboutUrl}
			keywords={[
				"Zen Typer about",
				"mindful typing app",
				"typing practice app",
				"Jonathan R. Reed typing app",
			]}
			breadcrumbItems={[
				{ name: "Mindful typing test", url: `${siteUrl}/` },
				{ name: "About Zen Typer", url: aboutUrl },
			]}
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<main class="content-page px-6 pb-32 pt-16 text-text">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-8">
				<nav aria-label="Primary" class="content-nav">
					<a href="/">Home</a>
					<a href="/quote/">Quote mode</a>
					<a href="/zen/">Zen mode</a>
					<a href="/contact/">Contact</a>
					<a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a>
					<a href="/subprocessors/">Subprocessors</a>
				</nav>

				<article class="glass content-prose rounded-2xl p-8 md:p-10 space-y-8 stagger-fade-in">
					<header class="space-y-4 border-b border-iris/15 pb-7">
						<p class="eyebrow text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.28em]">About</p>
						<h1>About Zen Typer</h1>
						<p class="!text-lg !leading-8">
							Zen Typer is a typing practice app built by Jonathan R. Reed. It tracks rhythm and accuracy without turning the session into a race.
						</p>
					</header>

					<section class="space-y-4">
						<h2>Why it exists</h2>
						<p>
							Most typing sites are built around speed runs and leaderboards. Zen Typer keeps the parts that actually help, quote prompts, WPM feedback, accuracy tracking, and session history, and leaves out the pressure. It works as a warmup before deep work, an accuracy drill, or a place to get words moving when the blank page is the problem.
						</p>
						<p>
							Quote Mode is the measured one: a short passage, immediate feedback on every character, and a WPM number at the end. Zen Mode is open. Words drift across the canvas as you commit them, and the session stats sit in the corner instead of in the way.
						</p>
					</section>

					<section class="space-y-4">
						<h2>Privacy and design principles</h2>
						<p>
							There is no account system behind Zen Typer, and no writing service. Settings, drafts, and session details are written to your browser and stay there. The interface is dark by default, works from the keyboard, and drops the animation it does not need when your system asks for reduced motion.
						</p>
						<p>
							The project stays small on purpose. Most updates go to accuracy, accessibility, and performance instead of new features.
						</p>
						<p>
							The public pages describe how the app works in plain text, so you can tell whether it fits before you open a session.
						</p>
					</section>
				</article>
			</div>
		</main>
	</body>
</html>
